":"podcast","title":null,"body":"Released in November 1970 and co-produced by Phil Spector, this album featured immersive production techniques. Unlike Spector's usual upbeat, high-energy productions, with Harrison, Spector \"brought things down, at least a little bit.\"","year":1970,"extra":null,"srcs":[{"source":"The Ongoing History of New Music","episode":"Dreampop - A History","url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=kFLMkvJAaUY"}]},{"kind":"podcast","title":null,"body":"The reissue of George Harrison's seminal album \"All Things Must Pass,\" originally released at the end of 1970, came with unique promotional items in 2021. For the new version, new promotional gnomes were made available, echoing the original album cover featuring Harrison with garden gnomes. \"New promotional gnomes were made available for the new version.\"","year":1970,"extra":null,"srcs":[{"source":"The Ongoing History of New Music","episode":"60 Mind-Blowing Things About Music: 2021 Edition","url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=-N6IM5Nm5iw"}]},{"kind":"podcast","title":null,"body":"This was George Harrison's debut triple album as a solo artist, released \"out of the gate\" after The Beatles' breakup. It was co-produced by Phil Spector and featured a strong backing lineup including Eric Clapton, Billy Preston, Gary Wright, Ringo Starr, and all four members of Badfinger. The album is regarded by some as possibly the best of the solo Beatles' initial releases and is particularly noted for Harrison's signature slide guitar. \"A triple album with Phil Spector as co-producer, an embarrassment of musical talent backing him up.\"","year":1970,"extra":null,"srcs":[{"source":"For the Record - The 70s","episode":"Ep. 32 - Take Me to Church - Religion in 70s Popular Music","url":"https://seventies.libsyn.com/ep-32-take-me-to-church-religion-in-70s-popular-music"}]},{"kind":"podcast","title":null,"body":"This was George Harrison's first post-Beatles solo album. It reached number one on the LP charts and included a version of Bob Dylan's \"If Not For You,\" which Harrison helped record and subsequently popularized. \"his first post-beetles solo album All Things Must Pass.\"","year":1970,"extra":null,"srcs":[{"source":"Hit Parade Music History and Music Trivia","episode":"Country Roads and Summer Nights Edition Part 1","url":"https://shows.acast.com/hit-parade/episodes/698fd3ebd6c27a06bb92e1f2"},{"source":"Hit Parade Music History and Music Trivia","episode":"Country Roads and Summer Nights Edition Part 1","url":null}]},{"kind":"podcast","title":"Reissue with Promotional Gnomes (2021)","body":"The reissue of George Harrison's seminal album \"All Things Must Pass,\" originally released at the end of 1970, came with unique promotional items in 2021. For the new version, new promotional gnomes were made available, echoing the original album cover featuring Harrison with garden gnomes. \"New promotional gnomes were made available for the new version.\"","year":1970,"extra":null,"srcs":[{"source":"The Ongoing History of New Music","episode":"60 Mind-Blowing Things About Music: 2021 Edition","url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=-N6IM5Nm5iw"}]},{"kind":"podcast","title":"Triple Album","body":"This was George Harrison's debut triple album as a solo artist, released \"out of the gate\" after The Beatles' breakup. It was co-produced by Phil Spector and featured a strong backing lineup including Eric Clapton, Billy Preston, Gary Wright, Ringo Starr, and all four members of Badfinger. The album is regarded by some as possibly the best of the solo Beatles' initial releases and is particularly noted for Harrison's signature slide guitar. \"A triple album with Phil Spector as co-producer, an embarrassment of musical talent backing him up.\"","year":1970,"extra":null,"srcs":[{"source":"For the Record - The 70s","episode":"Ep. 32 - Take Me to Church - Religion in 70s Popular Music","url":null}]}],"book_tracks":[{"id":167452234349515,"artist":"George Harrison","artist_id":131487512